Justification:
The function `ngx_http_script_copy_capture_code` checks for URI escaping conditions when `e->is_args` is set and the request URI contains a '+' or percent-encoded character. Specifically, in the snippet provided:

```c
if ((e->is_args || e->quote)
    && (e->request->quoted_uri || e->request->plus_in_uri))
{
    p = r->captures_data;

    return cap[n + 1] - cap[n]
           + 2 * ngx_escape_uri(NULL, &p[cap[n]], cap[n + 1] - cap[n],
                                NGX_ESCAPE_ARGS);
} else {
    return cap[n + 1] - cap[n];
}
```

Here, if `e->is_args` is true and the request URI contains '+' or percent-encoded characters (`quoted_uri` or `plus_in_uri` flags are set), it uses `ngx_escape_uri` to escape the captured data. The function `ngx_escape_uri` can convert one input byte into up to three output bytes (for example, converting a '%' character to "%25"). Therefore, if an attacker-controlled URI contains '+' or percent-encoded characters and `e->is_args` is set, it is possible for one input byte to become three output bytes.

